=== condvar p1 ===
wake_probe: build=condvar  post path takes a user mutex: yes
  kern.osproductversion 27.0.1
  hw.model       Mac14,9
  machdep.cpu.brand_string Apple M2 Pro
  cpus           10
  timebase       numer=125 denom=3

P1 no lost wakeup
  table A: 1000 posts with no waiter at all, then the consumer drains (timeout 5 ms)
  engine                           posted   consumed    verdict
  mach sem                           1000       1000         ok
  condvar, post takes mutex          1000       1000         ok
  condvar, post no mutex             1000       1000         ok
  table A cannot tell the two condvar variants apart: the counter survives either way,
  because nothing runs concurrently here and the signal is not needed to keep the count.

  table B: the natural rate.  150 bursts of 10 posts every 200000 ns, 2 ms of silence
  between bursts, waiter timeout 20 ms, waiter does a pseudo-random 0-200 us of work
  between waits so the phases drift.  latency = wake - max(post, wait start), so the
  waiter's own work is not charged to the primitive; late = 100 us or more.
  engine                            wakes timeouts     late  waited ms  delivery latency
  mach sem                           1500        0       12      549.4  p50     8.6 p99     73.2 max    7738.5 us
  condvar, post takes mutex          1500        0       24      543.9  p50     9.0 p99    197.1 max    1754.8 us
  condvar, post no mutex             1496        0       37      522.9  p50     7.8 p99    620.5 max    4024.3 us
  the mach and mutex rows are the noise floor of this metric on this machine (OS
  scheduling of a default-QoS waiter), so at the natural window width no lost wake
  was observed for any engine: the mechanism is below table C's resolution.

  table C: the window, hit by construction.  The waiter's check-then-block window is
  widened to 50000 ns in every engine and flagged, so the publisher posts inside it -
  exactly the situation a realtime callback reaches by chance.  50 iterations, a
  fresh instance each time, waiter timeout 20 ms.  lost = the waiter paid the whole
  timeout; reached = iterations in which the publisher really did post inside the
  window (the rest are excluded: the publisher was descheduled past it); post = how
  long the post itself took inside the window.
  engine                             lost  reached  post max  verdict
  mach sem                              0       49    4.916 us  ok
  condvar, post takes mutex             0       50   98.708 us  ok
  condvar, post no mutex               49       49    0.833 us  LOST WAKES
  verdict (mach sem, the primitive W1 adopts): PASS
exit=0
=== condvar p2 ===
wake_probe: build=condvar  post path takes a user mutex: yes
  kern.osproductversion 27.0.1
  hw.model       Mac14,9
  machdep.cpu.brand_string Apple M2 Pro
  cpus           10
  timebase       numer=125 denom=3

P2 publish latency: 200000 posts, no waiter, on a THREAD_TIME_CONSTRAINT_POLICY thread
  build=condvar, post takes a user mutex: yes
  nothing drains while the posts are timed, so the count climbs to 200000 and the instance
  is destroyed after: deliberate, a mach semaphore counts and signal cannot fail,
  which is exactly why this is a latency test and not a correctness test (that is p1).
  thread_policy_set(THREAD_TIME_CONSTRAINT_POLICY): (os/kern) successful (0)
  readback: (os/kern) successful (0) period=256000 computation=12000 constraint=24000 ticks (10.667/0.500/1.000 ms)
  pass criteria: p99 < 10.0 us and max < 50.0 us
  condvar, post takes mutex          p50     0.041  p99     0.041  max     5.166 us  (n=200000)  PASS
  verdict: PASS
exit=0
